1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,589
This is part 2 of the E C E 351 midterm review. Part 1, video 20 a, covered blocks 1 to 3: lexical analysis, program understanding, and automata.

2
00:00:14,039 --> 00:00:22,402
Part 2 starts with block 4, grammars and parsing. Then come the lab concepts, the exam questions, and the rule sheet.

3
00:00:22,852 --> 00:00:33,135
Block 4 is grammars and parsing, from Notes chapter 4 and the week 6 slides. It starts with a grammar: terminals, nonterminals and productions.

4
00:00:33,585 --> 00:00:42,715
A derivation uses the productions. It rewrites the start symbol, one nonterminal per step, until only terminals are left.

5
00:00:43,165 --> 00:00:50,739
A parse tree shows the derivation as a tree. If one string has two parse trees, the grammar is ambiguous.

6
00:00:51,189 --> 00:00:56,799
Recursive descent turns the grammar into code, with one method for each nonterminal.

7
00:00:57,249 --> 00:01:02,924
LL(1) tells us when one token of lookahead is enough to choose each production.

8
00:01:03,374 --> 00:01:12,441
The pre-midterm questions for this block are in questions-pre: section 1, section 3, and the short answers in 12.3.

9
00:01:12,891 --> 00:01:24,389
The week 6 slides say that the LL(1) proofs, with first, follow and predict sets, are not on this midterm. They are on the final. So that part is short here.

10
00:01:24,839 --> 00:01:34,077
A grammar has terminals. They are the tokens from the lexer, written in quotes, such as metric and the dot. A terminal is never rewritten.

11
00:01:34,527 --> 00:01:40,756
Nonterminals, such as DecimalNumber, start with a capital letter. Each one must be rewritten.

12
00:01:41,206 --> 00:01:49,953
Each line is a production: a left side, an arrow, and a right side. The bar starts another alternative for the same left side.

13
00:01:50,403 --> 00:01:55,203
The start symbol is the left side of the first production. Here it is Measurement.

14
00:01:55,653 --> 00:02:05,082
Now derive metric 3 point 875. Start with Measurement, and use its first production. The new symbols are orange.

15
00:02:05,532 --> 00:02:08,988
Rewrite DecimalNumber with Digits, dot, Digits.

16
00:02:09,438 --> 00:02:12,233
Rewrite the first Digits with the token 3.

17
00:02:12,683 --> 00:02:20,341
Rewrite the last Digits with 875. Only terminals are left, so the string is in the language.

18
00:02:20,791 --> 00:02:30,711
The rule: a derivation rewrites one nonterminal per step, with one production. A leftmost derivation always rewrites the leftmost one.

19
00:02:31,161 --> 00:02:44,324
Now try metric 3, 7 slash 8. After metric and 3, the grammar needs a dot, but the input has 7. No production fits. The derivation is stuck, so the string is rejected.

20
00:02:44,774 --> 00:02:53,115
Grammars come in classes. The smallest class is regular. A finite automaton recognizes it, for example x star.

21
00:02:53,565 --> 00:03:04,125
Next is LL(1). It needs a deterministic pushdown automaton. The stack adds recursion. That is question 12.3.1.

22
00:03:04,575 --> 00:03:21,919
Next is context-free. It needs a nondeterministic pushdown automaton. a to the n, b to the n, needs a context-free grammar, question 12.3.2. The posted answer says that LL(1) is also correct.

23
00:03:22,369 --> 00:03:30,519
a to the n, b to the n, c to the n needs a context-sensitive grammar, question 12.3.3.

24
00:03:30,969 --> 00:03:46,243
The rule from the notes: choose the simplest class that describes the language. Simpler classes are faster to parse. a to the n, b to the n needs a context-free grammar, because counting the a's needs a stack.

25
00:03:46,693 --> 00:03:58,917
Two grammars for x star. The first is right recursive: S goes to x S, or epsilon. The second is left recursive: S goes to S x, or epsilon.

26
00:03:59,367 --> 00:04:10,802
Both give the same strings: epsilon, x, x x, and so on. A python check compared all strings up to length 8. Same strings means weakly equivalent.

27
00:04:11,252 --> 00:04:16,777
The trees are different. For x x, the first grammar gives a tree that leans right.

28
00:04:17,227 --> 00:04:27,595
The second grammar gives a tree that leans left. Strongly equivalent needs the same strings and the same trees, so these two are only weakly equivalent.

29
00:04:28,045 --> 00:04:39,907
Question 12.3.8: when we refactor a grammar, the new grammar is usually only weakly equivalent. It accepts the same strings, but it builds different trees.

30
00:04:40,357 --> 00:04:52,879
Question 1.2.1 has a calculator grammar with precedence. Each level has its own nonterminal: Expr for plus, Term for times, Factor for the operands.

31
00:04:53,329 --> 00:05:02,055
Plus has the lowest precedence, so it is at the top level: Expr goes to Term plus Expr. In the tree, the plus is near the root.

32
00:05:02,505 --> 00:05:11,657
Times is one level lower. The right Expr becomes a Term, and Term goes to Factor times Term. The times is lower in the tree.

33
00:05:12,107 --> 00:05:16,032
Factor gives the operands, the integers 1, 2 and 3.

34
00:05:16,482 --> 00:05:24,269
Evaluate from the leaves up. The times is lower, so 2 times 3 is 6 first. Then 1 plus 6 is 7.

35
00:05:24,719 --> 00:05:37,433
The rule: one nonterminal per level, and the lowest precedence at the top. High precedence ends up near the leaves, so it is done first. A python check found exactly one tree.

36
00:05:37,883 --> 00:05:52,134
Two plus signs are grouped by the recursion. Expr goes to Term plus Expr is right recursive, so 1 plus 2 plus 3 groups as 1 plus, 2 plus 3. The next slides show associativity.

37
00:05:52,584 --> 00:06:04,808
A grammar is ambiguous if one legal string has two different parse trees. Take E goes to E plus E, or E times E, or int, and the string 1 plus 2 times 3.

38
00:06:05,258 --> 00:06:13,066
Tree 1 has the plus at the root. The times is lower, so 2 times 3 is done first: 1 plus 6 is 7.

39
00:06:13,516 --> 00:06:20,407
Tree 2 has the times at the root. Now 1 plus 2 is done first: 3 times 3 is 9.

40
00:06:20,857 --> 00:06:32,974
One string with two trees: the grammar is ambiguous. Question 12.3.4: to show that a grammar is ambiguous, give one string with two parse trees.

41
00:06:33,424 --> 00:06:53,733
Question 12.3.5: to show that a grammar is unambiguous is beyond E C E 351. We say that we cannot find such a string. And question 12.3.6: a context-free grammar can express ambiguity, a P E G cannot.

42
00:06:54,183 --> 00:07:04,103
Ambiguity counts trees, not values. 4 plus 5 plus 6 has two trees, and both give 15. So the grammar is ambiguous.

43
00:07:04,553 --> 00:07:12,084
Associativity decides the order when the same operator appears twice, as in 8 minus 6 minus 2.

44
00:07:12,534 --> 00:07:20,939
Minus is left associative, so the rule is left recursive: E goes to E minus F. Only the left side can grow.

45
00:07:21,389 --> 00:07:28,173
The tree leans left. 8 minus 6 is done first, which is 2. Then 2 minus 2 is 0.

46
00:07:28,623 --> 00:07:35,109
The exponent is right associative, so the rule is right recursive: E goes to F, caret, E.

47
00:07:35,559 --> 00:07:43,132
The tree leans right. 3 to the 2 is done first, which is 9. Then 2 to the 9 is 512.

48
00:07:43,582 --> 00:07:59,091
The rule: a left associative operator needs a left recursive rule. A right associative operator needs a right recursive rule. Only one side of the operator repeats the nonterminal, so each string has one tree.

49
00:07:59,541 --> 00:08:08,843
The dangling else is a classic ambiguity. The else is optional, so the grammar has two forms of cond: with an else, and without one.

50
00:08:09,293 --> 00:08:13,346
For if, if, s, else, s, tree 1 gives the else to the inner if.

51
00:08:13,796 --> 00:08:20,175
Tree 2 gives the else to the outer if. One string, two trees: the grammar is ambiguous.

52
00:08:20,625 --> 00:08:27,899
Three fixes change the grammar and remove the ambiguity: end if, a required else, or indentation.

53
00:08:28,349 --> 00:08:40,040
A note in the language specification, as in C and Java, says that the else goes with the inner if. It does not change the grammar, so the grammar stays ambiguous.

54
00:08:40,490 --> 00:08:52,757
Recursive descent writes a parser by hand from the grammar, with four rules. Rule 1: a nonterminal becomes a method. List and Name each get a method, and List calls Name.

55
00:08:53,207 --> 00:09:00,332
Rule 2: a terminal becomes a call to consume on the lexer. Name consumes an I D token.

56
00:09:00,782 --> 00:09:10,169
Rule 3: a repetition, star or plus, becomes a loop. Name star becomes a while loop that runs until the end of the input.

57
00:09:10,619 --> 00:09:15,760
Rule 4: an alternation, the bar, becomes an if. The next slides show it.

58
00:09:16,210 --> 00:09:22,994
The start method ends with consume E O F. It checks that no tokens are left after the list.

59
00:09:23,444 --> 00:09:32,276
A recognizer maps a string to true or false. A normal return means accept. An exception from the lexer means reject.

60
00:09:32,726 --> 00:09:47,190
Question 3.2.1 is an LL(1) calculator grammar. Here is the complete recognizer from the posted solution. S, Expr and Term call the methods of their right side, in order.

61
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:57,496
Factor has two alternatives, so it has an if. If the next token is an open parenthesis, it takes the second one. Else it consumes an Int.

62
00:09:57,946 --> 00:10:08,527
TermTail goes to plus Expr, or epsilon. The plus alternative starts with a token. So the if inspects for a plus, consumes it, and calls Expr.

63
00:10:08,977 --> 00:10:19,175
The epsilon alternative consumes nothing. So the if has no else. When the next token is not a plus, TermTail returns and consumes nothing.

64
00:10:19,625 --> 00:10:27,689
FactorTail has the same shape, with times in place of plus. It calls Term, so the times takes only the next Term.

65
00:10:28,139 --> 00:10:38,784
The evaluator has the same methods, and each one returns a value. Expr returns Term plus TermTail. Term returns Factor times FactorTail.

66
00:10:39,234 --> 00:10:47,298
For epsilon, TermTail returns 0, the identity of plus. So Term plus 0 is just the value of Term.

67
00:10:47,748 --> 00:10:52,079
For epsilon, FactorTail returns 1, the identity of times.

68
00:10:52,529 --> 00:11:09,894
FactorTail goes to times Term, so line 23 returns Term. Run it on 2 times 3 plus 4. Term returns 2 times 3, which is 6. Then TermTail adds 4: 6 plus 4 is 10. A python check ran this code.

69
00:11:10,344 --> 00:11:19,197
LL(1) means: scan the input from the left to the right, build a leftmost derivation, and use 1 token of lookahead.

70
00:11:19,647 --> 00:11:26,773
Measurement has two alternatives, so the code has an if. One token decides: metric or imperial.

71
00:11:27,223 --> 00:11:35,841
Here the lookahead is imperial, so the parser takes the second alternative. It consumes imperial and calls FractionalNumber.

72
00:11:36,291 --> 00:11:41,006
If no alternative matches, the method throws an exception: reject.

73
00:11:41,456 --> 00:11:50,480
A grammar is LL(1) when one token always picks the production. Then the recursive descent parser can be generated by a tool.

74
00:11:50,930 --> 00:11:59,357
The first shape that fails is a common prefix. S goes to u w, or u t. Both alternatives start with u.

75
00:11:59,807 --> 00:12:04,329
With the lookahead u, the if cannot choose. One token is not enough.

76
00:12:04,779 --> 00:12:13,803
Left factoring fixes it: consume the common prefix first, then decide. S goes to u R. R goes to w, or t.

77
00:12:14,253 --> 00:12:21,165
Now the two alternatives of R start with different tokens, w and t. One token decides.

78
00:12:21,615 --> 00:12:34,778
A prefix can hide behind a nonterminal, so look at what each alternative can start with, also through nonterminals. And the simple tests can only show that a grammar is not LL(1).

79
00:12:35,228 --> 00:12:44,529
The second shape that fails is left recursion. E goes to E plus T, or T. This is the natural way to write an expression grammar.

80
00:12:44,979 --> 00:12:55,731
Write the method E from the first alternative. Its first line calls E, before it consumes a token. The input never moves, so the calls never end.

81
00:12:56,181 --> 00:13:02,539
Also, both alternatives start with whatever T starts with. So one token cannot choose.

82
00:13:02,989 --> 00:13:11,287
The fix turns the recursion around. E goes to T E prime. E prime goes to plus T E prime, or epsilon.

83
00:13:11,737 --> 00:13:18,692
The strings stay the same, but the trees change. For 1 plus 2 plus 3, the old tree leans left.

84
00:13:19,142 --> 00:13:23,793
The new tree leans right. So the two grammars are only weakly equivalent.

85
00:13:24,243 --> 00:13:31,304
Both fixes change the parse tree. So the code that builds the A S T puts the left associativity back.

86
00:13:31,754 --> 00:13:46,858
The full test proves that a grammar is LL(1). The slides say it is not on this midterm, but it is on the final. Step 1 converts E B N F to B N F. This grammar is already in B N F.

87
00:13:47,308 --> 00:13:55,585
EPS asks: can the nonterminal derive epsilon? Only E prime can, with E prime goes to epsilon.

88
00:13:56,035 --> 00:14:06,297
First is the set of terminals that can start the nonterminal. T starts with int, so E and S also start with int. E prime starts with plus.

89
00:14:06,747 --> 00:14:18,608
Follow is the set of terminals that can come right after the nonterminal. After E comes dollar dollar. E prime is at the end of E, so its follow is also dollar dollar.

90
00:14:19,058 --> 00:14:29,746
After T comes E prime, so the follow of T has the first of E prime, plus. E prime can vanish, so add the follow of E prime: dollar dollar.

91
00:14:30,196 --> 00:14:41,673
Predict of a production is the first of its right side. If the right side can vanish, add the follow of the left side. So E prime goes to epsilon gets dollar dollar.

92
00:14:42,123 --> 00:14:58,699
E prime's two predict sets, plus and dollar dollar, are disjoint. The other nonterminals have one production each. So the grammar is LL(1). In the common prefix grammar, both predict sets were u. They overlap, so it is not LL(1).

93
00:14:59,149 --> 00:15:08,707
Epsilon and dollar dollar are different. Epsilon matches nothing. Dollar dollar is the end of input token, and the parser consumes it.

94
00:15:09,157 --> 00:15:19,461
Block 5 is the labs. The slides say that the midterm covers the labs up to Lab 4. We use only the slides and the notes, never lab code.

95
00:15:19,911 --> 00:15:30,919
The notes draw a compiler as a chain of stages. Lexing cuts the source string into tokens. Lab 1 does this with regular expressions. Block 1 taught them.

96
00:15:31,369 --> 00:15:39,198
Parsing builds the A S T from the tokens. Labs 1 and 3 use recursive descent for this. Block 4 taught it.

97
00:15:39,648 --> 00:15:46,944
Lab 2 is about transformations. A transformer reads a string, builds a tree, and writes another string.

98
00:15:47,394 --> 00:15:53,965
Lab 3 is about objects and equality. The question is: when are two A S Ts the same?

99
00:15:54,415 --> 00:16:02,713
Lab 4 is term rewriting, a kind of optimization. It rewrites the A S T with rules until no rule applies.

100
00:16:03,163 --> 00:16:14,406
A program transformation turns one form into another. The slides do it by syntax-directed translation: the grammar of the input guides the translation.

101
00:16:14,856 --> 00:16:23,773
Step 0: define one translation for each production, before you see any input. Here each production computes a value v.

102
00:16:24,223 --> 00:16:29,706
Step 1: parse the input. This is the parse tree of 1 plus 2 times 3.

103
00:16:30,156 --> 00:16:39,372
Step 2: start at the bottom and work up. Each Factor gives its number: 1, 2 and 3. A Term with only a Factor keeps that value.

104
00:16:39,822 --> 00:16:46,606
The Term for 2 times 3 multiplies: 2 times 3 is 6. The Expr above it keeps 6.

105
00:16:47,056 --> 00:16:54,587
Step 3: the root adds: 1 plus 6 is 7. The value at the root is the translation of the whole input.

106
00:16:55,037 --> 00:17:04,594
The regex to N F A templates are also a syntax-directed translation. The one exception in the course is the Lab 2 de-compiler.

107
00:17:05,044 --> 00:17:13,983
Lab 3 asks when two things are the same. The slides name three kinds of sameness: equals, isomorphic and equivalent.

108
00:17:14,433 --> 00:17:19,275
Equals: the same structure with the same names. These two machines are copies.

109
00:17:19,725 --> 00:17:26,787
Isomorphic: the same shape with other names. Rename P to A and Q to B, and the two machines match.

110
00:17:27,237 --> 00:17:36,922
Equivalent: the same language. The lower machine has 4 states, but it accepts the same strings: a, a b a, a b a b a, and so on.

111
00:17:37,372 --> 00:17:48,508
For expressions, equivalence is co N P complete, so it is expensive. The labs ask a SAT solver for an input on which the two expressions differ.

112
00:17:48,958 --> 00:18:07,134
For automata it is cheap. Minimize both machines, and equivalent becomes isomorphic. The 4 state machine minimizes to the P Q shape. In the same way, a followed by b a star, and a b star followed by a, give the same minimal D F A.

113
00:18:07,584 --> 00:18:22,389
Lab 4 is term rewriting. A rule replaces a pattern in the tree with a smaller tree. Our example has two rules: e plus 0 becomes e, and e times 1 becomes e. It is not lab code.

114
00:18:22,839 --> 00:18:27,405
Start with x times 1, plus 0. The tree has 5 nodes.

115
00:18:27,855 --> 00:18:34,020
The first rule matches the root: e plus 0 becomes e. The tree now has 3 nodes.

116
00:18:34,470 --> 00:18:39,313
The second rule matches: e times 1 becomes e. 1 node is left.

117
00:18:39,763 --> 00:18:45,459
No rule matches x. The rewriting is stuck. The notes call this point a fixed point.

118
00:18:45,909 --> 00:18:51,434
Why must it stop? The notes give three reasons. One: the input tree is finite.

119
00:18:51,884 --> 00:18:57,004
Two: every rewrite makes the program smaller. The notes call this monotonic.

120
00:18:57,454 --> 00:19:03,257
Three: the size has a bound. It cannot be negative. So the rewriting must stop.

121
00:19:03,707 --> 00:19:17,787
questions-pre is the course bank of past midterm questions. Chapter 1: derive a string from a grammar. Block 4 taught it. This video adds 1.1.2, where a derivation gets stuck.

122
00:19:18,237 --> 00:19:31,655
Chapter 2: program understanding. What does this print, the object diagram at a line, and polymorphic call targets. Block 2 taught them. This video adds one question about call targets.

123
00:19:32,105 --> 00:19:38,484
Chapter 3: write a recognizer, a parser or an evaluator. Block 4 taught and solved it.

124
00:19:38,934 --> 00:19:48,193
Chapter 4: automata. Most types are in blocks 1 and 3. This video solves running an automaton and designing a regex.

125
00:19:48,643 --> 00:19:58,883
Chapter 12: short answers. Block 4 answered the grammar questions. This video answers the automata questions, after the worked questions.

126
00:19:59,333 --> 00:20:08,911
questions-pre 1.1.2: derive five sentences from this grammar, or show that you cannot. Pause the video and try it.

127
00:20:09,361 --> 00:20:20,519
Engineer calculates design. The first word, Engineer, picks Technical. Technical gives Engineer, calculates, design. All three words match, so we accept.

128
00:20:20,969 --> 00:20:23,913
Physician examines patient goes through Medical.

129
00:20:24,363 --> 00:20:31,381
Judge decides case goes through Legal. Both derivations match every word, so both are accepted.

130
00:20:31,831 --> 00:20:43,245
Engineer cuts patient. Engineer picks Technical, so the next word must be calculates. The input has cuts. The derivation is stuck, so the sentence is rejected.

131
00:20:43,695 --> 00:20:53,913
Lawyer advocates design. Lawyer picks Legal. Lawyer and advocates match, but Legal ends with case, not design. Stuck again: reject.

132
00:20:54,363 --> 00:21:07,633
questions-pre 2.3.1, the last question: line 29 calls e dot toString. Is this call site monomorphic or polymorphic? Pause the video and try it.

133
00:21:08,083 --> 00:21:22,611
A call site is polymorphic when it can call more than one method at run time. Line 28 calls operator. It runs AndExpr, then OrExpr, then AndExpr: two targets, so it is polymorphic.

134
00:21:23,061 --> 00:21:36,629
Line 29 calls toString. Only BinaryExpr defines it, on line 8. AndExpr and OrExpr do not override it. So all three calls run BinaryExpr dot toString.

135
00:21:37,079 --> 00:21:51,841
One target, so in this program line 29 is monomorphic. This is our answer: the posted solution does not give one. Inside toString, on line 8, the call to operator is polymorphic again.

136
00:21:52,291 --> 00:21:59,523
The output: each loop prints the operator, then null, operator, null. For example and, then null and null.

137
00:21:59,973 --> 00:22:12,261
questions-pre 4.1: run machine 1 on a b c, a b, and a c b. Then run machine 2, which adds an epsilon edge, on a c. Pause the video and try it.

138
00:22:12,711 --> 00:22:23,463
Input a b c. a goes from 1 to 2. b goes back to 1. c goes to 2. The input ends in 2, an accepting state, so the machine accepts.

139
00:22:23,913 --> 00:22:30,121
Input a b ends in state 1. State 1 is not accepting, so the machine rejects.

140
00:22:30,571 --> 00:22:42,241
Input a c b. After a, the machine is in state 2. State 2 has no c edge, so c goes to the error state X. X never leaves, and the machine rejects.

141
00:22:42,691 --> 00:22:55,619
Machine 2 has an epsilon edge from 2 back to 1. Input a c: a goes to 2. The epsilon edge goes to 1 without input. Then c goes to 2, and the machine accepts.

142
00:22:56,069 --> 00:23:05,754
questions-pre 4.2.1: write a regex for real constants. Read the constraints first. Pause the video and try it.

143
00:23:06,204 --> 00:23:13,436
Take one constraint at a time. Unary minus, written as a tilde, may come first: tilde, question mark.

144
00:23:13,886 --> 00:23:19,070
At least one digit must come before the decimal point: zero to 9, plus.

145
00:23:19,520 --> 00:23:28,075
Case one: a point, then zero or more digits, then an optional exponent: E, an optional minus, and one or more digits.

146
00:23:28,525 --> 00:23:36,653
Case two: no point, but an exponent. The bar joins the two cases. So a constant has a point, an E, or both.

147
00:23:37,103 --> 00:23:49,369
Test it. These must pass: 0 point 0 1, 2 point 7 8 and so on, minus 1 point 2 E 12, 7 E minus 5, and 35 point.

148
00:23:49,819 --> 00:24:00,870
These must fail: 35 has no point and no E, point 5 has no digit before the point, 1 E has no exponent digits, and plus is not allowed.

149
00:24:01,320 --> 00:24:10,792
questions-pre 4.6. This N F A reads x in state 1. It can go to 2, where it is stuck, or to 3, where it accepts.

150
00:24:11,242 --> 00:24:15,487
Angelic non-determinism takes the good choice: state 3.

151
00:24:15,937 --> 00:24:20,076
Demonic non-determinism takes the bad choice: state 2.

152
00:24:20,526 --> 00:24:24,451
Arbitrary non-determinism can take either: 2 or 3.

153
00:24:24,901 --> 00:24:33,797
Two differences: an N F A can have epsilon edges, and two edges with the same label from one state, like the two x edges.

154
00:24:34,247 --> 00:24:42,909
Are N F As more powerful than D F As? No. The subset construction turns every N F A into a D F A.

155
00:24:43,359 --> 00:24:50,463
A regular language needs a finite automaton. It runs in linear time: one step per input letter.

156
00:24:50,913 --> 00:24:59,937
Can a regex recognize H T M L? No. H T M L has nested tags, and nested structure is not regular.

157
00:25:00,387 --> 00:25:10,840
Rule sheet, block 1. A compiler has five phases: lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, optimization, and code generation.

158
00:25:11,290 --> 00:25:18,159
A lexeme is the characters in the input. A token is its kind. The lexer drops the white space.

159
00:25:18,609 --> 00:25:25,073
Maximal munch: the lexer takes the longest match. So two greater-than signs are one token.

160
00:25:25,523 --> 00:25:32,371
The regex operators: concatenation, or, star, plus, question mark, and character classes.

161
00:25:32,821 --> 00:25:40,757
To design a regex, list the constraints, write one part for each, and test strings that must pass and must fail.

162
00:25:41,207 --> 00:25:51,725
Rule sheet, block 2. An object diagram has the P C, one stack frame per call with one slot per local, and heap objects with arrows.

163
00:25:52,175 --> 00:25:58,937
Aliasing: an assignment copies the arrow, not the object. Two names then point to one object.

164
00:25:59,387 --> 00:26:07,025
Mutation changes the object. Re-assignment moves the arrow. ImmutableList append returns a new list.

165
00:26:07,475 --> 00:26:15,603
final stops re-assignment. The compiler proves one assignment on every path. The object is not made immutable.

166
00:26:16,053 --> 00:26:21,173
A polymorphic call goes to the method of the object's class at run time.

167
00:26:21,623 --> 00:26:29,132
Linear search: an array or a linked list, a loop or recursion. Recursion adds one stack frame per call.

168
00:26:29,582 --> 00:26:37,241
Rule sheet, block 3. A D F A has one arrow per letter per state. A missing arrow goes to the error state.

169
00:26:37,691 --> 00:26:49,360
An N F A can have epsilon edges and several edges with one label. It accepts if some path accepts. The four templates turn a regex into an N F A.

170
00:26:49,810 --> 00:26:58,663
Subset construction: read a letter, then take the epsilon closure. A set is accepting if it has an accepting N F A state.

171
00:26:59,113 --> 00:27:07,711
Moore minimization needs the explicit error state. Start with accepting and not accepting, and split until nothing changes.

172
00:27:08,161 --> 00:27:16,971
The minimal D F A is unique. Minimize both machines to compare languages. A D F A can have up to 2 to the n states.

173
00:27:17,421 --> 00:27:24,163
Divisible by n: the states are the remainders, and bit b takes s to 2 s plus b, mod n.

174
00:27:24,613 --> 00:27:35,813
Rule sheet, block 4. A grammar has terminals, non-terminals, productions and a start symbol. A leftmost derivation replaces the leftmost non-terminal.

175
00:27:36,263 --> 00:27:39,527
Ambiguity: one string with two parse trees.

176
00:27:39,977 --> 00:27:44,670
Precedence: one non-terminal per level. The lower level binds tighter.

177
00:27:45,120 --> 00:27:52,779
Associativity: left recursion gives a left-associative operator. The dangling else is an ambiguity.

178
00:27:53,229 --> 00:28:00,461
Recursive descent: a method per non-terminal, consume per terminal, if per choice, and a loop per star.

179
00:28:00,911 --> 00:28:09,935
L L one: one token of lookahead picks the production. The full L L one proof is on the final, not on this midterm.

180
00:28:10,385 --> 00:28:15,291
Rule sheet, block 5. The midterm covers the labs up to Lab 4.

181
00:28:15,741 --> 00:28:23,869
Syntax-directed translation: one translation per production, parse, then apply the translations from the bottom up.

182
00:28:24,319 --> 00:28:28,629
Three kinds of sameness: equals, isomorphic and equivalent.

183
00:28:29,079 --> 00:28:36,289
Term rewriting stops: the input is finite, each rewrite makes it smaller, and the size has a bound.

184
00:28:36,739 --> 00:28:46,617
A recognizer gives a boolean, a parser an A S T, and an evaluator a value. The epsilon branch returns the identity element.

185
00:28:47,067 --> 00:29:00,059
Recap. Block 5 covers the labs up to Lab 4. Translation is syntax-directed. There are three kinds of sameness. Term rewriting stops because each rule makes the tree smaller.

186
00:29:00,509 --> 00:29:11,581
This video solved more exam types: a stuck derivation, the call targets, running an automaton, designing a regex, and the short answers on automata.

187
00:29:12,031 --> 00:29:15,892
Use the five rule sheets for a last check before the exam.

188
00:29:16,342 --> 00:29:27,051
The midterm is on Thursday, October 29, from 3 to 4 15 pm. Read each question, draw the picture, and check your answer. Good luck.
